  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nI'll implement a two-level optimization strategy that combines:\n   - Analytical optimization using calculus-based gradient projection on the step function space\n   - Adaptive multi-scale optimization with dimension refinement based on similarity search\n\nThe core idea is to:\n   - Translate the coefficient sequence optimization into a problem of minimizing the (\u221e-norm of the discrete convolution)/(L2-norm of the coefficients)\u00b2\n   - Develop a gradient-based algorithm that works directly with the coefficient space, preserving non-negativity constraints\n   - Use dimension switching during optimization, heuristically choosing lengths that match active coefficient patterns\n   - Incorporate both local and global searches by maintaining a library of representative sequences\n\nI'll name this approach Analytical Gradient Descent with Adaptive Dimension Refinement (AGARD). \nThe rationale is that step function minimization is essentially a discrete convolution problem, and \nby mapping the optimization landscape using gradient information from the convolution space, we can \neffectively explore the sequence guaranteeing progress toward the minimum without dead ends.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nLet A = (a_0, a_1, ..., a_{n-1}) be the normalized step function sequence. We seek to minimize:\n   f(A) = 2n * max_k{(A*A)_k} / (\u2211a_i)^2\n\nWhere k indexes the full convolution with length 2n-1.\n\nHowever, we note that minimizing this function is challenging due to the non-convex \nlandscape and coupling between coefficients. We interpret it as a product of three terms:\n   \u221d max_k(A_k) / (\u2211a_i)\u00b2\n\nNote that the original score is simply:\n   score = 2n * max(conv) / (\u2211a_i)\u00b2\n\nBut unlike the previous program which summed to \u221a(2n), our defensiveness requires \u2211a_i \u2265 0.01.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nWe assume a fixed starting length of 1024, but allow adaptive dimension switching. \nThe plan:\n   A. Initialization:\n      - Start with a Gaussian sequence normalized by its integral\n      - Check for parent sequence and normalize appropriately\n\n   B.Search Process:\n      7. Adaptive Dimension Selection:\n         We compute a sequence of possible lengths by finding the nearest power-of-2 multiples \n         of the original length (n_base=1024) that satisfy n > 256 and n < 2*10^6 (below which \n         convolution becomes too slow)\n\n      8. Reliable Gradient Computation:\n         For the current sequence:\n             Compute the full convolution using FFT for speed\n             Compute the \u221e-norm gradient by examining all convolution combinations\n             While maintaining non-negativity coefficients\n\n      9. dimensionRefinementHeuristicIf the sequence stabilizes, analyze its Fourier profile\n         and find similar length sequences in our reference library that might yield a lower score\n         Then perform an LP search on those sequences to escape local minimum\n\n      10. Step Function Transformation:\n         Treat the sequence as weights on a base function, and transform through convolution \n         by perturbing both the weights and the base function's representation\n\n      11. dimension reduction:\n         Preserve important invariants like total mass while reducing the number of coefficients,\n         but this requires maintaining a balance between sharpness and coefficient spreading\n\n      12. Censoring and Normalization:\n         Unlike previous strategies, we will not lightly clip values to [0,1000]. Instead, \n         we will normalize individually by the total when evaluating, to avoid biasing \n         toward more flat sequences\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\nThe default configuration will be:\n\n   - Primary length: fixed at 1024 (the previous optimizer's length)\n   - Secondary lengths: {512, 2048}\n   - initial population:\n        uniform: np.random.dirichlet(np.ones(256))\n        gaussian: np.exp(-0.5*(np.linspace(0,1,1024)-0.5)**2)\n        raised_cosine: 0.5*(1+np.cos(2*np.pi*np.linspace(0,1,1024)))\n        flat: 1 + 0.2*np.cos(2*np.pi*np.linspace(0,1,1024))\n\n   We will prioritize searches of different lengths, but explain how to switch dimensions:\n\n        a) If current sequence has converged on a particular length with a score under 1.5\n        b) Check our reference library for similar sequences on different lengths\n        c) If available, perform an LP-based optimization (using scipy) on the new length\n\n   We provide this plan to ensure improvement.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\nThe strategy must complete within 1800 seconds. We estimate:\n\n   - FFT convolution: O(n log n) per evaluation\n   - Gradient descent: O(n) steps for each update\n   - For n=1024, each evaluation takes ~0.1 seconds (this is estimated)\n\nWe structure time usage:\n\n   Phase 1: 600 seconds for initialization and learning rate calibration\n   Phase 2: 700 seconds for repeated refinement on current length(s)\n   Phase 4: 400 seconds for dimension switching evaluation\n   Phase 6: 100 seconds for fallback and saving \n\nWith a rolling deadline that monitors remaining time.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\nWe re-validate the candidate using the authoritative function:\n\n   score = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n\nAnd accept only nonnegative results. We must ensure the total sum is >=0.01.\n\nCriteria for output:\n   c) score < current_best or significantly better than any previous run\n   d) Positive/zero coefficients\n   e) No extrapolation beyond capability of the evaluator function for this dimension\n\nWe guarantee the output sequence is:\n   - Nonnegative\n   - Has length either 512, 1024, or 2048 (default)\n   - Sum >=10^{-2} for the normalization step\n\nIf we run out of time in the search phase, we return the sequence with the best score found.",
